# Story 2 — exploring an existing project: `▣`, `f` and history

Spec: [`../specs/node-editor-ui.md`](../specs/node-editor-ui.md) (draft 7).

**As** someone opening a project I didn't build, **I want** to walk through it one flow at
a time, follow branches into the flow, and get back to where I was, **so that** I can
understand how the image is made before changing it.

Mostly `examples/effects` (13 nodes): a background, three layers on a composite, and a
palette feeding colours to three nodes. Step 8 uses `examples/transform`, where one node
reaches the composite twice.

Screens are 80×24. Under each: what's **green** (the focus) and **magenta** (related).
Everything else is white on black. The project has no errors, so nothing is red.

## 1. Open the project

```sh
conduit edit examples/effects
```

{{start}}

The anchor is the first sink, `preview` (§4.2). Upstream, `preview.image` leads to `comp`,
and `comp`'s first connected input is `base`, so the flow is `bg → comp → preview`. The
three layers are `▣`: connected, but not drawn in this flow.

- Green: the `preview` box. Magenta: the conduit into it.
- No `(m)ove` or `(H)/(L)` slide: `preview` has no output, so it can't go into a
  conduit or swap with `comp` (§6.5).

## 2. Walk the flow

**`h`** moves upstream onto the conduit.

{{conduit}}

- Green: the line from `comp` to `preview`. Magenta: `out` on `comp`, `image` on `preview`.

**`h`** again, onto `comp`.

{{comp}}

- No slide: `bg` has no input to take `comp`'s place, and `preview` has no output.

**`j`** goes into the box, onto `base`; **`j`** again, onto `layers[0]`.

{{layer0}}

- Green: `▣─┼─` on `layers[0]`. Magenta: `layers[0]`.
- `(f)ocus` is the primary action (`⏎`) on a `▣`.
- `(J)later` but no `(K)earlier`: `layers[0]` is already first.

## 3. Follow a layer

**`f`** brings `layers[0]` into the flow. It's an input, so only the upstream side is
redrawn (§4.4): `comp` and `preview` stay, and `bg` is replaced by the path through
`logo_at`: `logo_at ← logo_tinted ← logo` (`logo_tinted`'s first input is `layer`, before
its linked param `black`).

{{flow_b}}

- Focus stays on `comp.layers[0]`, which now draws its line. `base` has become `▣`.
- The flow is now five boxes, wider than the screen. This was a flow change, so the view
  re-centres on the focus, stopping at the flow's right end (§4.5). `logo` is off the left
  edge; the line into `logo_tinted` shows the flow carries on.

**`h`** five times: onto the conduit, `logo_at`, the conduit, `logo_tinted`. Then **`j`**
**`j`** to `black`.

{{black}}

- `black` is `logo_tinted`'s colour param, linked to the palette, so it's an input row
  like any other (§3.1).
- Walking scrolls as little as possible: just enough to bring `logo` in as the box to
  spare on the left.

## 4. Follow the colour into the palette

**`f`**. Upstream of `logo_tinted` is now the path through `black`: the palette `brand`.

{{flow_c}}

- Re-centring on `logo_tinted.black` would scroll past the start of the flow, so the view
  stops at the start, with `brand` in view.
- `brand` has one row per colour per user, each with its swatch (§7.1): `primary` and
  `teal` are used by nodes outside the flow (`▣`), `sand` and `orange` aren't used, and
  `accent` is in the flow.
- `layer` on `logo_tinted` has become `▣`: `logo` is no longer drawn.

**`h`** **`h`** to `brand`, then **`j`** to `primary`.

{{primary}}

- Green: `─┼─▣` on `primary`. Magenta: `██ primary`.
- `(c)onnect to` on an output end connects this output to another input as well (§6.3).

## 5. Follow it downstream

**`f`**. `primary` is an output, so this time only the **downstream** side is redrawn:
`brand` stays, and everything to its right is replaced by the path through `bar`.

{{flow_d}}

- `bar.color` is the flow input: `bar` is a rect whose colour comes from the palette.
- `comp` and `preview` are now off the right edge, beyond `bar_inverted`.

## 6. Back and forward

**`[`** returns to the flow before the last redraw, with the focus where it was when you
left it.

{{back}}

**`[`** again would return to the `logo` flow with the focus on `logo_tinted.black`, and
once more to the start. **`]`** goes forward again.

## 7. Jump with find

**`/`**, type **`swsh`**.

{{find}}

**`⏎`**. `swatch_shifted` becomes the anchor and the flow is built around it (§4.2):
upstream through `swatch`, whose only connected input is its linked `color`, to
`brand.teal`; downstream through `swatch_at` to `comp.layers[1]` and `preview`.

{{flow_e}}

- Green: the `swatch_shifted` box. Magenta: its conduits in and out.
- Re-centred on `swatch_shifted`; `brand` is cut by the left edge.
- Only `(L)slide`. Sliding upstream would put `swatch_shifted` after `brand.teal`, a
  colour, which its `layer` input can't take. Downstream works: `swatch → swatch_at →
  swatch_shifted → comp` keeps every type.

## 8. One node, two places

In `examples/transform`, `turned` reaches `comp` twice: directly as `layers[0]`, and
through `grown` and `baked` as `layers[2]`.

```sh
conduit edit examples/transform
```

{{tr_start}}

**`h`** **`h`** to `comp`, **`k`** to its last row, the empty `layers` slot (§3.1),
**`k`** again to `layers[2]`, then **`f`**.

{{tr_flow}}

- `bar` is off the left edge; the line into `turned` shows the flow carries on.
- `turned` has two `out` rows: its users in id order, `comp` (`▣`) then `grown` (in the
  flow).
- `(K)earlier` but no `(J)later`: `layers[2]` is last.

Now both ends of the conduit `turned → comp.layers[0]` are on screen, `turned`'s first
`out` row and `comp`'s `layers[0]`, each drawn as `▣`, because a flow is a single path.

**`h`** six times to `turned`, then **`j`** **`j`** to its first `out` row.

{{tr_turned}}

- The view scrolls left just enough to keep a box to spare, so `bar` shows: it has two
  users, `slanted` (`▣`) and `turned` (the flow).
- Magenta: `out`, and `layers[0]` on `comp`, the other end, because `comp` is in the
  flow (§5.3). It's off screen at this scroll position.

**`f`**: downstream from `turned` is now the path through `comp.layers[0]`.

{{tr_flow2}}

## Result

| Step | Keys |
|---|---|
| Open, walk to `comp.layers[0]` | `h` `h` `j` `j` |
| Follow the layer | `f` |
| To `logo_tinted.black`, follow it | `h` `h` `h` `h` `h` `j` `j` `f` |
| To `brand.primary`, follow it | `h` `h` `j` `f` |
| Back | `[` |
| Find `swatch_shifted` | `/` "swsh" `⏎` |
| `examples/transform`: to `layers[2]`, follow it | `h` `h` `k` `k` `f` |
| To `turned`'s first `out`, follow it | `h` `h` `h` `h` `h` `h` `j` `j` `f` |

No files change: this story only looks.

## Spec gaps this story turned up

Decided in the spec (draft 7):

| Gap | Decision |
|---|---|
| `▣` was "connected to a node not in the flow", but `turned → comp.layers[0]` is `▣` with both nodes in the flow | `▣` means the conduit isn't drawn in the flow (§4.3) |
| `(c)onnect` on an output end had no meaning | `(c)onnect to`: connect this output to another input too (§6.2, §6.3) |
| Focus at start-up | On the anchor node (§4.2) |
| Off-screen boxes | No indicator; the conduit running off the edge is enough (§4.5) |
| `K` / `J` labels | `(K)earlier` / `(J)later`, each only when it can apply (§6.3) |
| What a history step is | A flow change; `[` restores the flow and the focus where you left it (§6.1) |
| How far the view scrolls | Re-centre after a flow change, as little as possible while walking, never past the flow's ends (§4.5) |
| `turned` shows two rows both labelled `out` | Kept plain; the status line, magenta and `f` tell them apart (§3.1) |
